Source: bbq-predictor
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
# Creates the bbq-predictor system account from the sysusers.d snippet
# at install time, so the unit has a fixed user to run as.
               dh-sequence-installsysusers,
               qt6-base-dev,
               qt6-positioning-dev,
               libgl-dev,
               pkg-config
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: bbq-predictor
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
# The SQLite driver is loaded at run time, so it appears in no ELF
# header and dpkg-shlibdeps cannot see it. Without this the archive
# cannot be opened at all, which is the whole of what the timer does.
         libqt6sql6-sqlite
# Where systemd is not running, cron runs the fetch instead of the timer.
Recommends: cron | cron-daemon
Description: weather applet and forecast-verification archive
 A Qt Widgets applet that draws high-resolution temperature and rain
 graphs from several providers at once, and answers whether an afternoon
 is worth lighting a fire for.
 .
 It keeps a permanent archive of what was observed and what was
 forecast, scores each provider against the measurements as they arrive,
 and corrects later forecasts by the bias it has measured. The package
 also installs a systemd timer that fetches on its own clock, so the
 archive advances and verification accumulates without anybody opening
 the window.

Package: bbq-predictor-server
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
# The publisher is bbq-predictor --publish; this serves what it writes,
# which can as well be a tree published elsewhere and copied here.
Recommends: bbq-predictor
Description: server for the bbq-predictor weather archive
 A small daemon that answers bbq-predictor clients from the signed files
 the publisher writes: a fixed 48-byte request, and a prebuilt file held
 in memory in reply. There is no HTTP and no TLS; every file is signed
 with Ed25519 and checked by the applet, and the daemon never holds the
 signing key.
 .
 It binds its ports as root, then chroots into the published tree as an
 account of its own. The init script runs under sysvinit and OpenRC, and
 the service is not started on install: configure the ports in
 /etc/default/bbq-predictor-serve, then start it.
